### Spectrum of Allowed Boundary Modes

In the boundary–interior framework, particles are not fundamental objects but
*harmonics* of the global spherical boundary. Each harmonic is defined by its
anchor configuration, phase‑gradient structure, and the interior response
required to sustain it. A mode is observable in spacetime only if it can
propagate without encountering immediate congestion. This naturally leads to a
finite, discrete spectrum of allowed boundary modes.

#### Boundary complexity and interior congestion

A boundary pattern with many anchors, steep gradients, or multiple interfering
sub‑modes induces strong interior congestion. The interior is not passive: it
must support the boundary pattern through its bulk response. The more complex
the boundary harmonic, the more interior tension it generates, and the more
likely it is to encounter a configuration that the update operator cannot
propagate smoothly.

This explains why heavy particles are short‑lived. Their boundary modes are
high‑curvature, high‑tension configurations with many opportunities for local
steepening. They frequently hit congestion before they can stabilize into a
spacetime‑projectable mode.

#### Congestion as a filter on the particle spectrum

A mode becomes observable only if it survives the boundary’s congestion filter.
If a harmonic encounters congestion, it must shed structure and relax into a
simpler configuration. The resulting mode may itself be unstable, leading to a
sequence of congestion‑driven simplifications,

$$
A \;\rightarrow\; B \;\rightarrow\; C \;\rightarrow\; \text{stable}.
$$

The particles we observe are the harmonics that survive this process. They are
the stable or metastable modes whose anchor geometry and phase gradients can be
propagated smoothly for long enough to appear in spacetime.

This explains why the Standard Model contains only a small number of particle
families. These are the harmonics that the boundary can sustain without
immediate collapse.

#### Why supersymmetric partners do not appear

Supersymmetric partners would require boundary patterns with additional anchors,
higher‑order gradients, and more internal interference modes. Such harmonics
would induce far greater interior congestion than the already‑unstable heavy
quarks and bosons. As a result, they would hit congestion almost immediately and
shed structure before they could stabilize.

In this framework, the absence of supersymmetric particles is not a mystery or a
failure of symmetry. It is a geometric consequence of the boundary’s inability
to sustain those harmonics. SUSY modes collapse before projection, leaving only
their lower‑complexity decay products.

#### Spacetime as the projection of sustainable harmonics

Spacetime does not contain all possible boundary modes. It contains only those
harmonics that:

- have manageable curvature,
- avoid runaway steepening,
- couple weakly to the fixed chiral tilt,
- and can propagate without immediate congestion.

These are the modes that survive long enough to be projected into spacetime as
particles. Everything else collapses in the boundary before it can appear.

#### Summary

The observed particle spectrum is the set of boundary harmonics that survive the
congestion filter. Heavy or highly complex patterns decay before they can be
projected, while simpler harmonics persist. This naturally explains the limited
particle families of the Standard Model and the absence of supersymmetric
partners: only a small subset of boundary modes are dynamically sustainable.


### Decay as Congestion

In this framework, decay is not a mysterious force or a spontaneous event. It is
a geometric response of the boundary to a configuration it cannot propagate
smoothly. A boundary harmonic drifts through many configurations as its anchors
move, its phase gradients rotate, and its internal sub‑modes interfere. Most of
these configurations are smooth enough for the update operator to handle.

But occasionally, the interference of sub‑modes, combined with the fixed chiral
tilt and the local curvature of the boundary, produces a region where the phase
gradient becomes too steep. The boundary cannot update this region without
introducing discontinuities. This is **congestion**.

When congestion occurs, the boundary must shed the part of the pattern that
cannot be maintained. The shedding process relaxes the boundary into a simpler
harmonic. The decay products are precisely these simpler sub‑patterns that can
propagate outward without further congestion.

A decay chain is therefore a sequence of congestion‑driven simplifications,

$$
A \;\rightarrow\; B \;\rightarrow\; C \;\rightarrow\; \text{stable}.
$$

Each arrow corresponds to a moment when the drifting harmonic encounters a
locally unsustainable configuration. The final stable particle is the harmonic
that the boundary can propagate indefinitely without further congestion.

This geometric picture unifies particle decay, nuclear decay chains, atomic
cascades, and even black‑hole evaporation under a single mechanism: **boundary
congestion and harmonic simplification**.

### Why Half‑Life is Exponential

Half‑life emerges naturally from the geometry of congestion. A drifting boundary
mode explores a large space of configurations as its anchors move and its
internal modes interfere. Congestion occurs only when the instantaneous
configuration aligns in a particular way that produces a locally steep gradient.
This alignment is rare and depends only on the current state of the pattern, not
on its history.

Because congestion is:

- rare,
- memoryless,
- and dependent only on the instantaneous configuration,

the probability of encountering a congestion point in any small time interval is
constant. This leads directly to the exponential decay law,

$$
P(t) = e^{-t/\tau}.
$$

The half‑life is therefore not a built‑in timer or an intrinsic property of the
particle. It is the statistical shadow of how often a drifting harmonic happens
to encounter a configuration that the boundary cannot propagate smoothly.

This explains why particles with more complex boundary patterns—those with more
anchors, stronger curvature, or more internal interference—have shorter
half‑lives. Their harmonics explore a larger configuration space with more
opportunities for steepening, and thus more opportunities for congestion.

The exponential decay law is simply the macroscopic signature of this geometric
process.

### Chiral Tilt and Mode Drift

The update operator on the boundary contains a small, fixed chiral asymmetry. In
geometric terms, this acts like a tiny global tilt or directional bias on the
sphere. It does not change in time and does not depend on the particle’s
configuration. It is a structural property of the boundary dynamics.

Two‑anchor systems such as neutral kaons are sensitive to this tilt because
their phase‑gradient patterns are free to drift and rotate. The kaon system has
two independent harmonics,

$$
K^0 : (d,\;\bar{s}), \qquad 
\bar{K}^0 : (\bar{d},\;s),
$$

which correspond to opposite orientations of the same two‑anchor pattern. These
modes interfere on the boundary, producing a drifting composite pattern whose
phase gradient continually moves into and out of alignment with the fixed chiral
tilt.

When the phase gradient aligns with the tilt, the boundary can propagate the
pattern slightly more easily. When misaligned, propagation is slightly hindered.
This produces a time‑dependent coupling between the drifting kaon pattern and
the fixed chiral asymmetry.

The alignment–misalignment cycle is the geometric origin of kaon oscillations.
The small asymmetry in how the boundary handles the two orientations leads to a
slight preference in one direction of the oscillation. This is the geometric
origin of CP violation.

When the drifting pattern and the chiral tilt momentarily reinforce each other
in a way that produces a locally steep gradient, congestion occurs. The boundary
then sheds structure, leading to decay. The rare CP‑violating decay channels
arise from these special alignment conditions.

In this view, chiral tilt and mode drift are not abstract algebraic features but
geometric interactions between a fixed global asymmetry and a drifting boundary
harmonic.
